<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> A Walk in the Neighborhood</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's the Best!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: text: Interpret simple school notices. communication: Facilitate a simple discussion among classmates.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can interpret simple school notices using gestures to indicate main ideas (e.g., 'The notice says there's a park trip.' and points to a park picture). Can help facilitate a discussion on favorite places in the neighborhood by asking simple questions and using visual aids (e.g., 'What do you like at the park?' while showing a picture of a playground or pointing to a specific part of the map). Can guide classmates in expressing opinions about neighborhood places by using gestures (e.g., points to school and says, 'This school is the best.').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards or large pictures of different neighborhood places (park, school, street, playground, building with a tree and flowers). The teacher asks students to identify them: "What is this place?" (e.g., "It's a park!"). Students repeat the words. The teacher then asks, "What is your favorite place?" and points to pictures, encouraging simple responses like "Park!" or "I like the park!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher shows a simple, visual "school notice" about a "Neighborhood Fun Day at the Park!" (e.g., a drawing of a park with a 'FUN DAY!' sign and kids playing). The teacher models how to interpret it and explain it to a "classmate" (e.g., a puppet or another teacher). "Look! This notice says: 'Fun Day at the Park!' It's the <b>best</b>! We can <b>play</b> and <b>explore</b> the <b>playground</b>." The teacher uses gestures, pointing to the words and pictures, and emphasizes the /b/ sound in 'best' and the /f/ sound in 'fun' and 'favorite'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of 'best' and 'notice'.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'best' very good or not good?" (Very good!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does a 'notice' give us information?" (Yes!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we read a notice to learn something?" (Yes!)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "A Walk in the Neighborhood".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, colorful "Neighborhood Discovery Club" school notice or poster (genre: informational poster/notice, register: informal, format: illustrated poster) describing an activity to identify favorite places in the neighborhood. The notice includes sentences like: "Let's explore our <b>neighborhood</b>!", "Find your <b>favorite place</b>: the <b>park</b>, the <b>street</b>, a big <b>building</b>, or the <b>playground</b>?", "Tell your friends: 'This is the <b>best</b> place <b>because</b> it is <b>fun</b>!'". It uses images for "park," "street," "building," and "playground," and might include small pictures of "trees" and "flowers."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Mediation: text: Interpret simple school notices. communication: Facilitate a simple discussion among classmates.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> The teacher asks, "What is this notice about?" (Our neighborhood places). "Does it want us to walk and explore?" (Yes!).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> The teacher reads simple statements, and students say "True" or "False" by pointing to the notice or using hand gestures.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The notice says to find your favorite place." (True)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The notice says the park is bad." (False!)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"You can tell your friends why you like a place." (True)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Superlatives ('best'), Simple present + conjunction ('I like this park because it is fun.'), Question structure ('What is your favorite?').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> neighborhood, park, place, tree, flower, building, street, path, playground, walk, explore, visit, see, enjoy, like, share, fun, exciting, beautiful, quiet, friendly, busy, safe, favorite, best, cool, amazing, big, small, crowded.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> /b/ in 'best', /f/ in 'favorite'. Phonemic awareness: blending /b/ and /ɪg/ for 'big', 'bigger', 'biggest'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Describing preferences ('This is my favorite.'), Expressing opinions ('I like this park because it is fun.'), Discourse marker ('because').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sociolinguistic:</b> Explaining opinions ('I like this street because it has beautiful trees.').</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Interpreting Notices (Pair Work):</b> The teacher provides pairs of students with simple mini-notices (e.g., "Clean the Park Saturday!", "School Library is the best!") and corresponding pictures. Students take turns explaining what their notice says to their partner, using gestures. E.g., Student A: (points to "Clean the Park") "This notice says 'Clean the Park!' We clean the park on Saturday." Student B: "Oh, okay!" Teacher emphasizes using /b/ for 'best'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Explaining Favorites:</b> The teacher gives students sentence starters and visuals of neighborhood places. In pairs, students practice expressing preferences.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"My <b>favorite</b> place is the [park] <b>because</b> it is [fun]." (Teacher models 'favorite' with /f/ sound)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"This [school] is the <b>best</b> [building] <b>because</b>..." (Teacher models 'best' with /b/ sound)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The [park] is <b>big</b>. The [playground] is <b>bigger</b>. The school is the <b>biggest</b>." (Teacher explicitly works on /b/ and /ɪg/ blending).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Facilitating Questions:</b> Students work in new pairs. One student shows a picture of a neighborhood place, explains why it is their favorite, and then asks their partner, "What is your <b>favorite place</b> here? Why?" The partner responds using "because." The teacher encourages them to use gestures (e.g., pointing to the picture) to facilitate understanding.</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "A Walk in the Neighborhood".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activity: "Virtual Neighborhood Guide" Project Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each student shares their "Virtual Neighborhood Walk" project (their labeled online map, printout, or drawing of their map) created in the 21st Century Skills Project. They take turns acting as a "neighborhood guide" for their group, mediating the information on their map.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each student presents their map, pointing to their labeled favorite spots (park, school, playground, street, building, trees, flowers).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They interpret their labels and explain why each spot is their favorite. For example: "This is my <b>favorite park</b> <b>because</b> it has big <b>trees</b> and a <b>fun playground</b>. It is the <b>best</b>!" (Emphasizing /f/ in 'favorite' and 'fun', /b/ in 'best', and using 'big'.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They use gestures to indicate places and ideas, just like modeling.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After presenting, the "guide" facilitates a short discussion by asking their group members simple questions about their map: "What do you like in my neighborhood?" or "What do you think is the <b>best place</b> on my map?" or "Is this <b>building big</b> or small?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Group members listen, ask simple questions, and express their opinions using 'because'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, offering support and encouraging the use of target language.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students continue their "Virtual Neighborhood Guide" presentation in their small groups. The teacher observes specific students or groups using an observation checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Interprets simple labels/notices on their map using gestures (e.g., points to 'park' and says "This is the park.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Facilitates discussion by asking simple questions to classmates (e.g., "What is your favorite part?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (e.g., neighborhood, park, playground, favorite, best, fun, beautiful).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses superlatives ('best') and simple present with 'because' to explain opinions (e.g., "This is the best because it is fun.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation of /b/ (in 'best', 'big') and /f/ (in 'favorite', 'fun') is intelligible.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempts to use 'big,' 'bigger,' 'biggest' when describing places.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Engages classmates in a simple discussion about their maps.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick self-reflection before leaving the classroom.</p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can I tell my friends about my <b>favorite places</b> using "because"? (Yes / A little / No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can I ask my friends "What is your <b>favorite place</b>?" and understand their answer? (Yes / A little / No)</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was the most interesting place you learned about in our <b>neighborhood walk</b> today from your friends?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy or difficult about explaining your <b>favorite places</b> to your friends and asking them questions?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of mediation (interpreting notices/labels, explaining information, facilitating simple discussions, using gestures) worked well during the project presentations?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the A1.2 language structures (superlatives, simple present + because, question structure) used effectively by most students? How can I encourage more consistent use?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students successfully connect their virtual maps to verbal explanations and simple interactions? What adjustments are needed for future project-based mediation tasks?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How well did students use and pronounce 'best', 'favorite', 'big', 'bigger', 'biggest'?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the theme "It's the Best!" and the "Virtual Neighborhood Walk" project. The next lesson will introduce a new theme, scenario, and associated linguistic competences.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>